RULE NUMBER
004

TITLE
Forge: Generate outputs.proto for a resource (observable outputs only)

ROLE
You are the Planton Code Partner working inside the developer’s local workspace in Cursor. This rule creates `outputs.proto` for a resource to capture observable outputs. Do not create/modify other files.

SCOPE
- Detect provider and kind.
- Do not manually create folders; writer tool handles directories.
- Generate `outputs.proto` defining `<Kind>StackOutputs` with non-secret observable outputs only.
  (The FILE is `outputs.proto`; the MESSAGE name stays `<Kind>StackOutputs` -- the message name
  is the identity every downstream consumer keys on.)

TOOLS
- Reader: `_rules/component/_scripts/outputs_reader.py`
- Writer+Build: `_rules/component/_scripts/outputs_write_and_build.py`
- Reference components (live ground truth): `catalog/aws/awsvpc/v1alpha1/outputs.proto`
  and `catalog/aws/awsalb/v1alpha1/outputs.proto` -- canonical examples of observable, non-secret outputs.

SEQUENTIAL STEPS
1) Read: `python3 _rules/component/_scripts/outputs_reader.py --provider <provider> --kindfolder <kindfolder>`
2) Draft: Following the reference component `outputs.proto` files (and the skeleton below), author `outputs.proto` content accordingly.
3) Write+Build: `cat <<'PROTO' | python3 _rules/component/_scripts/outputs_write_and_build.py --provider <provider> --kindfolder <kindfolder> --stdin | cat`
   - If `build_succeeded` is false, refine content and retry (≤6).

OUTPUT
- Show created/updated path and key JSON fields from tools.
- On success: confirm file path. On error: brief fix and rerun (≤6).

SUCCESS
- Correct path, syntax, and package.
- Practical, non-secret outputs only.
- Writer tool reports `build_succeeded: true`.

OUTPUT STYLE IN CHAT
- Show a concise tree of the created/updated path and fields added.
- Show the non-interactive command(s) to run.
- If successful: confirm the file/folder path to inspect.
- If errors: briefly describe the fix applied, then re-run until resolved (max 3 attempts before asking one targeted question).

EXAMPLE SKELETON (adapt to actual schema; do not copy verbatim)
```proto
syntax = "proto3";

package dev.planton.aws.awscloudfront.v1alpha1;

// AwsCloudFrontStackOutputs captures observable identifiers from CloudFront.
message AwsCloudFrontStackOutputs {
  // distribution id
  string distribution_id = 1;
  // distribution domain name (e.g., d123.cloudfront.net)
  string domain_name = 2;
  // hosted zone id for alias records
  string hosted_zone_id = 3;
}
```

NOTES
- Secrets belong in external secret managers, not in stack outputs. When the provider
  offers a managed-secret indirection, export the secret's ARN/identifier instead of the
  material (e.g. a managed master password exported as its Secrets Manager ARN).
- Narrow exception -- provider-minted credentials with NO vault indirection: when the
  cloud itself mints a credential at create time, offers no managed-secret handle for it,
  and the stack output is the only wire to the consuming application (e.g. an IAM user's
  secret access key, a Cognito app client secret), export it. Mark it clearly: a
  "Sensitive -- treat as a credential" sentence in the proto field comment, and
  `sensitive = true` on the Terraform output. Never apply this exception to material the
  MODULE generates (keys/PEMs the module creates do not belong in outputs at all).
- Keep field names stable; other modules/tests reference these outputs downstream.
- A resource's live STATUS or health is never an output. Outputs are identity and wiring
  surfaces (ids, hostnames, endpoints, ARNs); a status captured at apply time goes stale
  the moment the cloud changes it (a member fails, a cluster upgrades, a pool scales), and a
  stale "active" in stored outputs is worse than no value. Health belongs to whoever reads
  it live -- the E2E verifier asserts it from the API, a console reads it on demand. The
  same holds for volatile metrics (utilization, timestamps) and for member/child ids that
  churn by design (autoscaled droplets): consumers address such fleets through tags or
  selectors, not through an output that is wrong an hour later.
- Export the ids of IMPLICIT sub-resources that other kinds target, constructed from
  the parent's id when the provider offers no readback -- ARM/cloud APIs often hang
  capabilities off a sub-resource nothing materializes explicitly (e.g. a storage
  account's `blobServices/default`, where diagnostic settings must point to capture
  data-access logs, while the account-level id exposes only account metrics). A
  consumer that cannot reach the real telemetry/grant scope by reference is a
  composition gap identical to a missing output.
- Map-typed outputs (`map<string, string>`, e.g. per-subnet mount-target IDs) are
  first-class: both engines emit them as nested objects, the flattener dot-flattens
  each entry (`field.some-key = value`), and the transformer routes the remaining
  path segments back into the proto map with the key VERBATIM -- so map keys may
  freely contain hyphens and dots (subnet IDs, object paths). Always add a
  `pkg/outputs/conformance_test.go` case that includes the map fields in
  `mustPopulate`; the conformance case is the only guard proving the map shape
  round-trips (an un-cased map field can silently arrive empty in the platform's
  typed outputs).